    "lawCode": "Add §332, Hway L",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S262\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to authorizing variable\nmessage signs placed within the right of way of highways to display\ncertain voting related communications\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill authorizes the commissioner of the Department of Transporta-\ntion (\"DOT\") and local municipalities to place variable message signs\nalong highways that display election and voting related messages during\ncertain times of the year.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one adds a new section 332 to the Highway Law providing the\nDepartment of Transportation Commissioner with authority to place vari-\nable message boards with election and voting related messages along any\nhighway under which the state has jurisdiction. The term \"variable\nmessage boards\" is defined within the bill. Such signs could display\nmessages including, without limitation:\n\n(1) notice of the deadline to register to vote beginning five days\nbefore such deadline; and\n\n(2) notice of any upcoming primary, general, or special election for any\nstatewide or local election beginning three days before said election\nand including said day.\n\nThe section also authorizes any political subdivision within the state\nto place variable message signs displaying the above referenced voting\nrelated on local highways as well, subject to the restrictions above.\n\nThe commissioner of DOT and local municipalities shall consult with the\nState Board of Elections prior to displaying voter related communi-\ncations to ensure such communications are uniform and accurate.\n\nThe bill also provides DOT with flexibility to give priority to any\nother message sign communications, including, but not limited to emer-\ngency alerts, amber alerts, missing vulnerable adult alerts, as well as\nother transportation related messages.\n\nSection 2 provides that no message shall be displayed if it interferes\nwith federal law or regulation, or results in the reduction of federal\naid highway funds.\n\nSection 3 provides for the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill would authorize the state and local municipalities to display\nvoting notification related signs during key times in an election cycle.\nNew York's voter participation rates continue to lag behind other states\nin the union. This proposal will increase voter awareness at a minimal\ncost.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: 56805 (Myrie) /A9706 (Arroyo)\n\nS.7369 of 2018 sponsored by Senator Dilan\n\n1/10/2018 - referred to Transportation committee\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal; the equipment to display such signs is already owned by the\nstate and local political subdivisions.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law. The department of transportation and state board of\nelections are both authorized to promulgate any rules and regulations\nnecessary to implement the provisions of this act on its effective date\nor before such date.\n",
